            "raw_output": {
              "output": "<path>/home/u26136/miyabi/notebooks/pd/pd02_app/code/problem.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Making a Pure Path Tracer from Scratch\n2: \n3: # Problem\n4: \n5: In this assignment, I implement **path tracing**, a core algorithm in computer graphics, from scratch in C++ without external libraries.\n6: The target scene is a Cornell box (a box-shaped interior) with:\n7: \n8: - sphere objects (one mirror, one diffuse)\n9: - a small triangle mesh object\n10: - an area light (an emissive patch on the ceiling)\n11: \n12: The renderer outputs a PPM image, and image noise decreases as the number of samples per pixel (spp) increases.\n13: \n14: This is a realistic application and is a good baseline for later parallelization (multicore CPU and GPU), where performance improvements can be measured clearly.\n15: \n16: # How the algorithm/baseline code works\n17: \n18: The implementation is in `code/pathtracer.cc`. The main steps are:\n19: \n20: 1. **Scene construction**\n21:    - The Cornell box is built from triangles (2 triangles x 5 faces; the front side is open for the camera).\n22:    - A ceiling emissive patch is added as an area light.\n23:    - Two spheres and several triangles are added as scene objects.\n24: \n25: 2. **Intersection tests**\n26:    - Spheres: analytic ray-sphere intersection\n27:    - Triangles: Moller-Trumbore ray-triangle intersection\n28:    - The nearest valid hit is selected\n29: \n30: 3. **Path tracing (pure path tracing)**\n31:    - For each pixel, the code traces `spp` camera rays with jittered sampling.\n32:    - At each hit, the next direction is chosen based on the material:\n33:       - diffuse: cosine-weighted hemisphere sampling\n34:       - mirror: perfect specular reflection\n35:       - light: add emitted radiance and terminate the path\n36:    - The path is traced across multiple bounces while updating throughput.\n37:    - Russian roulette is applied at deeper bounces.\n38: \n39: 4. **Output and verification metrics**\n40:    - Linear RGB is gamma-corrected (2.2) and written as PPM (P6).\n41:    - `render_ms` (computation time) is printed.\n42:    - `checksum` (sum over all pixel channels) is printed for reproducibility checks.\n43: \n44: Execution parameters are configurable from the command line:\n45: \n46: - `--width`, `--height`: image resolution\n47: - `--spp`: samples per pixel\n48: - `--max-depth`: maximum path depth (bounces)\n49: - `--seed`: random seed (fixed seed gives deterministic behavior)\n50: - `--output`: output PPM filename\n51: \n52: By changing these parameters, runtime can be scaled from seconds to a few minutes.\n53: \n54: # Where is parallelism\n55: \n56: This baseline has abundant data parallelism:\n57: \n58: - **Pixel parallelism**\n59:   - Final pixel colors are independent.\n60:   - On CPU, work can be split by rows or tiles.\n61: \n62: - **Sample parallelism**\n63:   - Samples within the same pixel are also independent.\n64:   - They can be combined by reduction (averaging).\n65: \n66: - **Intersection-level parallelism (future extension)**\n67:   - Intersection checks against many objects can be parallelized.\n68:   - Adding a BVH can reduce computation and improve memory behavior.\n\n(End of file - total 68 lines)\n</content>",
              "metadata": {
                "preview": "# Making a Pure Path Tracer from Scratch\n\n# Problem\n\nIn this assignment, I implement **path tracing**, a core algorithm in computer graphics, from scratch in C++ without external libraries.\nThe target scene is a Cornell box (a box-shaped interior) with:\n\n- sphere objects (one mirror, one diffuse)\n- a small triangle mesh object\n- an area light (an emissive patch on the ceiling)\n\nThe renderer outputs a PPM image, and image noise decreases as the number of samples per pixel (spp) increases.\n\nThis is a realistic application and is a good baseline for later parallelization (multicore CPU and GPU), where performance improvements can be measured clearly.\n\n# How the algorithm/baseline code works\n\nThe implementation is in `code/pathtracer.cc`. The main steps are:\n\n1. **Scene construction**",
                "truncated": false,
                "loaded": [],
                "display": {
                  "type": "file",
                  "path": "/home/u26136/miyabi/notebooks/pd/pd02_app/code/problem.md",
                  "text": "# Making a Pure Path Tracer from Scratch\n\n# Problem\n\nIn this assignment, I implement **path tracing**, a core algorithm in computer graphics, from scratch in C++ without external libraries.\nThe target scene is a Cornell box (a box-shaped interior) with:\n\n- sphere objects (one mirror, one diffuse)\n- a small triangle mesh object\n- an area light (an emissive patch on the ceiling)\n\nThe renderer outputs a PPM image, and image noise decreases as the number of samples per pixel (spp) increases.\n\nThis is a realistic application and is a good baseline for later parallelization (multicore CPU and GPU), where performance improvements can be measured clearly.\n\n# How the algorithm/baseline code works\n\nThe implementation is in `code/pathtracer.cc`. The main steps are:\n\n1. **Scene construction**\n   - The Cornell box is built from triangles (2 triangles x 5 faces; the front side is open for the camera).\n   - A ceiling emissive patch is added as an area light.\n   - Two spheres and several triangles are added as scene objects.\n\n2. **Intersection tests**\n   - Spheres: analytic ray-sphere intersection\n   - Triangles: Moller-Trumbore ray-triangle intersection\n   - The nearest valid hit is selected\n\n3. **Path tracing (pure path tracing)**\n   - For each pixel, the code traces `spp` camera rays with jittered sampling.\n   - At each hit, the next direction is chosen based on the material:\n      - diffuse: cosine-weighted hemisphere sampling\n      - mirror: perfect specular reflection\n      - light: add emitted radiance and terminate the path\n   - The path is traced across multiple bounces while updating throughput.\n   - Russian roulette is applied at deeper bounces.\n\n4. **Output and verification metrics**\n   - Linear RGB is gamma-corrected (2.2) and written as PPM (P6).\n   - `render_ms` (computation time) is printed.\n   - `checksum` (sum over all pixel channels) is printed for reproducibility checks.\n\nExecution parameters are configurable from the command line:\n\n- `--width`, `--height`: image resolution\n- `--spp`: samples per pixel\n- `--max-depth`: maximum path depth (bounces)\n- `--seed`: random seed (fixed seed gives deterministic behavior)\n- `--output`: output PPM filename\n\nBy changing these parameters, runtime can be scaled from seconds to a few minutes.\n\n# Where is parallelism\n\nThis baseline has abundant data parallelism:\n\n- **Pixel parallelism**\n  - Final pixel colors are independent.\n  - On CPU, work can be split by rows or tiles.\n\n- **Sample parallelism**\n  - Samples within the same pixel are also independent.\n  - They can be combined by reduction (averaging).\n\n- **Intersection-level parallelism (future extension)**\n  - Intersection checks against many objects can be parallelized.\n  - Adding a BVH can reduce computation and improve memory behavior.",
                  "lineStart": 1,
                  "lineEnd": 68,
                  "totalLines": 68,
                  "truncated": false
                }
              }
            },
